-## Setting Up TA for Your Team
-
-TA distinguishes between **shared configuration** (checked into VCS, reviewed as normal PRs) and **local runtime state** (machine-specific, should be ignored by your VCS). This section explains the split, how to configure your VCS ignore rules, and how to optimize staging for large workspaces.
-
-### Shared vs Local Files
-
-**Commit these to VCS** — they encode team policy and agent definitions:
-
-| File / Directory | Purpose |
-|---|---|
-| `.ta/workflow.toml` | Staging strategy, verify commands, channel config |
-| `.ta/policy.yaml` | Action governance and auto-approval rules |
-| `.ta/constitution.toml` | Behavioral contract for agents |
-| `.ta/memory.toml` | Persistent memory backend config |
-| `.ta/agents/` | Agent configuration files |
-| `.ta/constitutions/` | Constitution documents |
-| `.ta/templates/` | Project templates |
-
-**Never commit these** — they are local runtime state and change on every machine:
-
-| File / Directory | Why not commit |
-|---|---|
-| `.ta/staging/` | Agent workspaces (gigabytes on game projects) |
-| `.ta/goals/` | Goal run history and state files |
-| `.ta/events/` | Event stream log |
-| `.ta/daemon.toml` | Machine-local daemon configuration |
-| `.ta/audit-ledger.jsonl` | Audit log (environment-specific) |
-| `.ta/velocity-stats.jsonl` | Per-machine velocity stats |
-| `.ta/release-history.json` | Release tracking (written by `ta release run`) |
-| `.ta/plan_history.jsonl` | Phase-transition history (timestamps differ per developer) |
-
-To audit the current split for your project:
-
-```bash
-ta plan shared
-```
-
-### Generating VCS Ignore Rules
-
-Run this once per project clone to add the TA block to your VCS ignore file:
-
-```bash
-ta setup vcs                        # auto-detect the VCS, update .gitignore / .p4ignore / svn:ignore
-ta setup vcs --dry-run              # preview what would change without writing
-ta setup vcs --dry-run --json       # machine-readable detection, evidence and changes
-ta setup vcs --vcs svn              # force a VCS (git, svn, perforce, none, or a plugin name)
-ta setup vcs --force                # rewrite the TA block (e.g. after upgrading TA)
-ta setup vcs --project-type rust    # pre-populate [commit] auto_stage with Cargo.lock
-ta setup vcs --project-type node    # pre-populate with package-lock.json
-ta setup vcs --project-type python  # pre-populate with poetry.lock / Pipfile.lock
-ta setup vcs --project-type go      # pre-populate with go.sum
-```
-
-`ta setup vcs` also auto-detects lock files on disk and adds them to `[commit] auto_stage` in `.ta/workflow.toml` (if it exists). This ensures that lock files regenerated during the verify step are always committed alongside the code change that triggered them.
-
-For Git projects, this appends a block to `.gitignore`:
-
-```
-# Trusted Autonomy — local runtime state (do not commit)
-.ta/daemon.toml
-.ta/daemon.local.toml
-.ta/staging/
-.ta/goals/
-.ta/events/
-...
-```
-
-For Perforce projects, it writes the same entries to `.p4ignore`. If `P4IGNORE` is not set, TA prints a reminder:
-
-```
-⚠ Perforce: P4IGNORE env var is not set.
-  TA wrote local-only paths to .p4ignore, but Perforce won't use it until:
-    export P4IGNORE=.p4ignore   (add to your shell profile)
-```
-
-### How `ta setup vcs` picks the VCS (detection evidence)
-
-One detector is shared by `ta init`, `ta setup vcs` and `ta doctor`. It recognizes git, Subversion (`svn`), Perforce, VCS adapter plugins, and `none`. Every run prints the markers that led to the choice, so you can see why:
-
-```
-VCS: git (detected)
-  evidence: .git at .git: .git directory present in the project root
-```
-
-A marker in the project root beats one in an enclosing directory, which beats an environment variable (`P4PORT`, `P4CLIENT`; the value is never printed or stored). Within the same level the order is git, svn, perforce, plugin. Markers that were found but lost are listed as `also found (not used)`. In an empty tree the output lists everything that was checked.
-
-For scripts and installers, `--json` prints one document instead of text. It is safe with `--dry-run`:
-
-```bash
-ta setup vcs --dry-run --json
-ta init --json
-```
-
-```json
-{
-  "schema_version": 1,
-  "dry_run": true,
-  "forced": false,
-  "source": "detected",
-  "kind": "git",
-  "plugin": null,
-  "detection": {
-    "schema_version": 1,
-    "kind": "git",
-    "plugin": null,
-    "evidence": [
-      { "kind": "git", "plugin": null, "marker": ".git", "path": ".git",
-        "reason": ".git directory present in the project root" }
-    ],
-    "ignored_evidence": [],
-    "checked": [".git", ".svn", ".p4config"]
-  },
-  "ignore": {
-    "target": ".gitignore",
-    "action": "would_update",
-    "added_entries": [".ta/staging/"],
-    "preserved_unrecognized": [],
-    "note": null
-  },
-  "workflow_changes": [],
-  "notes": []
-}
-```
-
-- `kind` is `git`, `svn`, `perforce`, `none` or `plugin` (then `plugin` holds the plugin name).
-- `evidence` lists what supports the chosen kind; `ignored_evidence` lists markers for other kinds that lost.
-- Paths use forward slashes on every platform and are relative to the project root; a marker found in an enclosing directory is an absolute path, and an environment variable appears as `env:P4PORT`.
-- `source` is `override` when `--vcs` was given. `detection` still shows what was found.
-- `ignore.action` is one of `created`, `updated`, `unchanged`, `would_create`, `would_update`, `skipped`. `ta init --json` wraps the same document as its `vcs` field, next to `project`, `already_initialized`, `created` and `skipped`.
-- The JSON Schemas are `schema/vcs_setup_report.schema.json`, `schema/vcs_detection.schema.json` and `schema/init_report.schema.json`.
-
-**Subversion.** `ta setup vcs --vcs svn` adds TA's local paths to the `svn:ignore` property of the `.ta` directory. `svn:ignore` has no comment syntax, so TA has no block to own: it only ever appends missing patterns and never removes, reorders or rewrites a line you already have. The change is a working copy modification you commit yourself. `.ta` must be under version control first (`svn add --depth=empty .ta`); if it is not, or `svn` is not installed, TA says so, changes nothing and exits non-zero.
-
-**VCS adapter plugins.** A plugin can declare detection markers in its `plugin.toml`:
-
-```toml
-[detect]
-markers = [".hg"]
-```
-
-When one of those exists in the project root and no built-in marker does, the kind is `plugin`. TA does not know a plugin's ignore format, so it writes no ignore file and prints what to add by hand. A plugin cannot take the names `git`, `svn`, `perforce` or `none`.
-
-**Re-running.** `ta setup vcs` only touches the TA block of the ignore file: everything else in the file, including its line endings (CRLF stays CRLF), is left exactly as it was. If the block is present but out of date (for example it predates `.ta/setup-progress.json`), a plain run refreshes it in place and lists the entries it added; `--force` is only needed to rewrite a block that is already complete. `ta init` runs the same step every time, including on an already-initialized project, and reports what changed.
-
-**`--force` never silently drops lines it doesn't recognize.** If you've manually added a `.ta/`-prefixed entry to your ignore file that isn't one of TA's own known paths, `ta setup vcs --force` preserves it as-is and prints a warning instead of removing it:
-
-```
-⚠ Found 1 unrecognized .ta/-prefixed line(s) in .gitignore that are not in
-  LOCAL_TA_PATHS/SHARED_TA_PATHS (crates/ta-workspace/src/partitioning.rs):
-      .ta/some-real-file.jsonl
-  Preserved as-is — not removed. If this protects a real TA runtime file,
-  register it in LOCAL_TA_PATHS so future `--force` runs regenerate it consistently.
-```
-
-### Team Onboarding Workflow
-
-When a teammate clones the repo for the first time:
-
-```bash
-git clone <repo>
-cd <project>
-ta setup vcs          # add local state to .gitignore
-ta setup wizard       # generate .ta/workflow.toml, policy.yaml, etc.
-ta doctor             # verify everything looks healthy
-ta daemon start       # start the local TA daemon
-```
-
-Policy and constitution changes work like any other code change — agents propose them via draft, the team reviews and merges via the normal PR flow.
-
-### Large-Workspace Staging (Smart Mode)
-
-By default (`strategy = "full"`), `ta goal start` copies the entire project into a staging directory. For large workspaces (Unreal Engine, Node projects with `node_modules/`, etc.) this can be slow or impractical.
-
-**Smart mode** symlinks excluded directories instead of copying them. Only the agent-writable subset is physically copied:
-
-```toml
-# .ta/workflow.toml
-[staging]
-strategy = "smart"    # symlink .taignore / excluded dirs
-```
-
-With a `.taignore` or `DEFAULT_EXCLUDES` (includes `node_modules/`, `target/`, etc.):
-
-```
-Staging: 55 MB copied, 749 GB symlinked (smart mode)  (13,636× reduction) in 0.3s
-```
-
-The agent sees the full project layout. Files behind symlinks are read-only (any write attempt will be flagged). Files in the agent-writable area are fully staged and diffed normally.
-
-**Windows ReFS Dev Drive**: On a Windows machine with a ReFS-formatted Dev Drive, use `strategy = "refs-cow"` for instant zero-cost clones via Copy-on-Write. Falls back to `smart` automatically on NTFS volumes.
-
-```toml
-[staging]
-strategy = "refs-cow"   # Windows ReFS only; auto-falls back to "smart" on NTFS
-```
-
-**`ta doctor` staging check**: `ta doctor` warns when `strategy = "full"` and the workspace exceeds 1 GB:
-
-```
-  Staging strategy... full (workspace is 42 GB — consider strategy=smart with a .taignore)
-    Add to .ta/workflow.toml: [staging]\nstrategy = "smart"
-```
-
