+
+## Feature Velocity Stats
+
+`ta stats` tracks per-goal timing, outcomes, and agent token costs. Data is recorded automatically when goals complete (applied, denied, cancelled, or timed out by GC).
+
+**Two files, two purposes:**
+
+| File | Location | Purpose |
+|------|----------|---------|
+| `velocity-stats.jsonl` | `.ta/` (gitignored) | Per-machine raw log — written on every goal terminal state |
+| `velocity-history.jsonl` | `.ta/` (committed) | Shared cross-machine log — auto-migrated on every `ta draft apply` |
+
+Each entry in `velocity-history.jsonl` carries a `machine_id` (first 8 hex chars of SHA-256(hostname)) and a `committer` (from `git config user.name`). Multi-machine appends are unique lines that merge without conflicts.
+
+Migration is automatic — every `ta draft apply` promotes local entries into the committed history file before making the VCS commit, so the file stays in sync without any manual step.
+
+### Derived titles (Meridian)
+
+When [Meridian](#effort--kpi-analytics-meridian) is installed, `ta draft apply --git-commit` calls `meridian summarize-title` on the goal's objective (its first user message) and stores the result in the `derived_title` field alongside the plain `title` field in `velocity-history.jsonl`. The plain `title` field always remains authoritative — `derived_title` is `null` when Meridian isn't installed or the call fails, and is meant to give Meridian's reporting a more consistent, concise label to group goals by.
+
+### Token cost tracking
+
+Each goal records the tokens the agent used, in four kinds: uncached input, output, cache read and cache write (a long goal's input is mostly cache reads, so leaving them out understates the cost badly). The cost is the agent runtime's own reported cost when it gives one (exact), otherwise it is computed from a price table (an estimate). A model the table does not know, such as an Ollama or Codex model, keeps its tokens and shows $0.00 with no estimate flag: TA never invents a price. See "Tracking spend by origin" below for how to read, price and cap it.
+
+The COST column appears automatically in `ta stats velocity` and `ta stats velocity-detail` when at least one entry has a non-zero cost.
+
+### Tracking spend by origin
+
+Spend can be reported, and capped, by why the work started. Each goal carries an **origin class**:
+
+| Class | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `asked` | A person asked for this work |
+| `suggested` | Someone or something suggested it and it was accepted |
+| `unasked` | Nobody asked (the default, so unclassified spend is never hidden) |
+
+The class is accounting data only. It never changes whether a draft can be auto-approved, what policy applies, where a goal is routed or what an agent may do. Goals whose origin is `cos` or `chat` are still never auto-approved, whatever their class.
+
+**Setting it.** Whoever launches the goal sets the class, in one of three ways (the flag wins over the provenance file, which wins over the environment):
+
+```bash
+ta run "Fix the login redirect" --origin-class asked
+TA_GOAL_ORIGIN_CLASS=suggested ta run "Tidy the changelog"
+ta run "Triage the inbox" --provenance-file wake.json    # {"requester": {...}, "origin_class": "unasked"}
+```
+
+An unknown value is refused with the accepted ones listed. The class is stamped when the goal is created and never changes afterwards (a later `ta run --goal-id` cannot rewrite it). Goals started from inside a running goal inherit its class through `TA_GOAL_ORIGIN_CLASS`.
+
+**An agent cannot set it.** The `ta_goal_start` tool, the chat-mode tools and the daemon API do not accept an origin class, and a `ta run` started from inside an agent ignores a different `--origin-class` and keeps the inherited one (with a warning that says so). Honest limit: that last check reads the agent marker variables (`TA_AGENT_CONTEXT` and friends), and an agent that unsets them could start a nested goal with another class. Until the OS sandbox phases land, treat the class as accounting you trust the launcher for, not as a security boundary.
+
+**The completion record.** When a goal's agent session ends, TA publishes one record in three places:
+
+```bash
+ta goal status <id> --json        # under "completion"
+curl -s 'http://127.0.0.1:7700/api/events?types=goal.completed'   # a goal.completed event per goal
+# headless runs: the __TA_HEADLESS_RESULT__ line carries it under "completion"
+```
+
+```json
+{
+  "goal_id": "3f2a9c10-...", "state": "pr_ready", "model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
+  "input_tokens": 86, "output_tokens": 44216,
+  "cache_read_tokens": 4423499, "cache_creation_tokens": 138618,
+  "usd": 1.4391539, "cost_estimated": false, "cost_source": "runtime",
+  "origin": "cos", "origin_class": "asked",
+  "aborted": false, "outcome": null
+}
+```
+
+`cost_source` is `runtime` (the agent runtime reported the cost: exact, `cost_estimated` is false), `table` (priced from the table: an estimate, `cost_estimated` is true) or `none` (no price: tokens only, `usd` is 0).
+
+**Spend report.** `GET /api/spend` groups spend across the goals the daemon serves. It is read-only and returns only totals, so it needs no human credential:
+
+```bash
+curl -s 'http://127.0.0.1:7700/api/spend?group_by=origin_class,project&since=2026-10-01&until=2026-11-01'
+```
+
+`group_by` takes any of `origin_class`, `project`, `origin`, `model` (empty gives one total). `since` and `until` take a date or an RFC 3339 time and filter on when the goal was created. Each group carries token totals of all four kinds, `usd`, and the count of goals with an exact cost (`exact_cost_goals`), an estimated cost (`estimated_cost_goals`) and no price (`unpriced_goals`), plus `aborted_budget_goals`. The numbers are read from the goal records, not from the velocity file, because an agent can write to the velocity file. Goals recorded before cost tracking existed count as unpriced.
+
+**Prices.** The built-in table covers the Claude 3, 4 and 5 families, with cache read at one tenth of the input price and cache write at 1.25 times it. The `claude-sonnet-5-5`, `claude-opus-5-5` and `claude-fable-5-1` rows are provisional (they use their tier's price and have not been checked against a published price sheet), which is one reason the runtime's reported cost is preferred whenever it exists. To set your own, create `.ta/pricing.toml`; its rows are checked before the built-in table:
+
+```toml
+[[model]]
+name = "claude-sonnet-5-5"   # matches any model name containing this text
+input = 3.0                  # USD per million tokens
+output = 15.0
+cache_read = 0.30
+cache_write = 3.75
+
+[[model]]
+name = "my-local-model"
+exact = true                 # match the whole model name only
+input = 0.0
+output = 0.0
+cache_read = 0.0
+cache_write = 0.0
+```
+
+All four rates are required. An optional `cache_write_1h = <USD per million>` sets the 1 hour cache-write rate, which otherwise defaults to twice `input`. A malformed file is reported in the log and the built-in table is used.
+
+**Capping a goal.** `--max-tokens` and `--max-usd` stop the agent when the goal reaches the ceiling; `[budget] max_tokens` and `max_usd` in `.ta/workflow.toml` set project defaults that the flags override:
+
+```bash
+ta run "Large refactor" --headless --max-tokens 5000000 --max-usd 20
+```
+
+```toml
+[budget]
+max_tokens = 5000000   # input + output + cache read + cache write
+max_usd = 20.0
+```
+
+When a ceiling is reached TA stops the agent, prints which ceiling and what was used, records the outcome `aborted_budget` on the goal, and builds the draft from the partial work, marked as incomplete (`ta draft view` says so first). What it can promise: TA reads the runtime's usage report after each agent turn, so the agent is stopped at the first turn boundary after the ceiling is crossed. Overshoot is possible by up to one turn (a single long turn can cost a lot); undershoot is capped, since a goal is never stopped before it reaches the ceiling. Ceilings are enforced only for headless runs, because that is where TA reads the usage report. `--max-usd` needs a price: from the runtime's reported cost or the price table. For an unpriced model it warns and only `--max-tokens` applies. A goal that finishes on its own over the ceiling is not marked incomplete.
+
+### Spend across goals and interactive sessions
+
+`GET /api/spend` only sees TA goals, but long interactive Claude Code and OMP sessions are often the larger bill. `ta cost report` totals all of them in one table:
+
+```bash
+ta cost report                                  # by source
+ta cost report --since 2026-10-01 --group-by day,source
+ta cost report --group-by model --project myapp
+ta cost report --group-by context_bucket --json
+```
+
+It reads three sources, each optional and found automatically: this project's TA goals, Claude Code sessions in `~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl`, and OMP sessions in `~/.omp/agent/sessions/**/*.jsonl`. Use `--claude-dir` and `--omp-dir` to read from somewhere else. A source that is not there is named in the header as missing; it is not an error.
+
+`--group-by` takes a comma-separated list of `day`, `source`, `model`, `project`, `context_bucket` and `origin_class` (the last applies to TA goals; other rows show `n/a`). `--since` and `--until` take a date or an RFC 3339 time (UTC), and `--project` keeps projects whose name contains the text.
+
+Example output (trimmed):
+
+```
+Cost report
+Sources:
+  ta_goals     missing: no .ta/goals directory here (run from a TA project root)
+  claude_code  found: 3970 file(s), 53949 turn(s) kept, 48777 duplicate row(s) folded, 85 synthetic skipped  [~/.claude/projects]
+  omp          found: 1 file(s), 335 turn(s) kept, 0 duplicate row(s) folded, 0 synthetic skipped  [~/.omp/agent/sessions]
+Costs: 335 exact, 17672 estimated, 36277 unpriced (unpriced models count tokens, cost 0)
+
+source / model                      turns   sess     input    output  cache-rd  cache-wr        usd  cost
+claude-sonnet-5-5 / claude_code      8661   2437     18.3k     5.32M  1644.13M    115.41M    1249.03  8661 est
+claude-sonnet-5-5 / omp               335      1       702    194.0k    43.45M    700.1k       9.09  335 exact
+
+By context size (prompt tokens per turn), per source:
+source / bucket           turns   sess  ...  cache-rd        usd  cost
+50-100k / claude_code     10682   2061  ...   663.28M     695.18  5111 est, 5571 unpriced
+>400k / claude_code       17832     16  ...  11707.21M   1857.21  2311 est, 15521 unpriced
+```
+
+How the numbers are made:
+
+- Claude Code writes an assistant message once per streamed block, all with the same `message.id`. TA keeps the last row for each id, so a message counts once, and skips `<synthetic>` rows. The header says how many duplicates were folded.
+- OMP's own per-message cost is used and marked **exact**. Claude Code costs are **estimated** from the price table (`.ta/pricing.toml`, then the built-in table); where a transcript splits cache writes by lifetime, the 1 hour part is priced at twice the input rate. A model with no price row is **unpriced**: its tokens are counted, its cost is 0 and nothing is invented. Add a row to `.ta/pricing.toml` to price it.
+- The context-size table groups turns by prompt size (input plus cache-read plus cache-creation tokens): 0 to 50k, 50 to 100k, 100 to 200k, 200 to 400k and over 400k. A long session that keeps re-reading a large context shows up as most of its cost in the top rows. TA goals are many turns each and are not in this table.
+
+### Exporting the cost report to Meridian
+
+To analyse cost against your KPIs, write the same data to a file [Meridian](#effort--kpi-analytics-meridian) can read:
+
+```bash
+ta cost report --since 2026-10-01 --meridian cost.jsonl
+meridian analyze --source jsonl --path cost.jsonl
+```
+
+`--meridian <file.jsonl>` writes one record per TA goal or interactive session, using the same sources, filters and de-duplication as the report (a session whose transcript repeats a message counts once). After the report it prints the path, how many records were written and the exact `meridian` command to read the file.
+
+Each record carries `id`, `title`, `timestamp`, `tokens_input` (uncached input only), `tokens_output`, `source` and, for goals, `phase`. Meridian keeps these string fields as metadata: `cost_usd`, `cost_exact` (`true` only when every turn's cost was reported rather than estimated), `model` (the model with the most tokens), `cache_read_tokens`, `cache_write_tokens`, `context_peak` (largest prompt in the session; `n/a` for goals) and `workload` (`goal` or `interactive`). Cache reads are kept out of `tokens_input` on purpose: they are most of an interactive session's cost, and folding them in would swamp the effort score.
+
+Re-running is safe. Records are matched by `id` (`<source>:<session or goal id>`), so a second run updates them in place instead of adding copies, and any other lines already in the file are kept. The file is written to a temporary file and renamed into place, so an interrupted run cannot leave it half-written. If the existing file has a line that is not valid JSON, nothing is changed and the error names the line. With `--json`, the confirmation goes to stderr so stdout stays valid JSON.
+
+### Testing a cost optimization on identical work (golden set)
+
+A golden entry is a finished goal saved as its start commit, its prompt and its result. Replaying it with one setting changed shows what that setting does to cost and outcome on the same work, without a large multi-arm grid.
+
+```bash
+# 1. Save a finished goal. --check is a command that exits 0 when a result is acceptable.
+ta golden capture 98a823ac --check "cargo test -p mycrate"
+
+# 2. See what you have (baseline cost, age, replay count).
+ta golden list
+
+# 3. Replay it with an override. Prints a cost estimate first.
+ta golden replay fix-the-thing-98a823ac --set optimizers.input.use=none --tag no-optimizer --max-usd 5
+
+# 4. Compare every replay with its baseline.
+ta golden report fix-the-thing-98a823ac
+```
+
+`capture` refuses a goal that started from uncommitted changes, because a replay starts from the clean commit and could differ for that reason alone. Pass `--allow-dirty` to capture it anyway. It also refuses a goal that is unfinished, stopped on a budget ceiling, or has no recorded start commit.
+
+`replay` checks the start commit out into a scratch git worktree and runs a normal `ta run` there, so your working tree, branch and index are never touched. The scratch worktree is removed afterwards. Values given with `--set key=value` are read as JSON where they parse (`3`, `true`, `"x"`) and as plain text otherwise. The replay's draft is never applied for you: review it with `ta draft list` and close it when done. Only committed files are replayed; uncommitted work in your tree is not part of the replay.
+
+`report` shows cost, tokens, outcome, whether the check passes and how similar the diff is, per replay. The verdict is:
+
+| Verdict | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `equivalent` | The check did not fail and the diff is at least 80% similar to the baseline's. With no check defined, this rests on diff similarity alone. |
+| `differs` | The run finished and the check did not fail, but the diff is less than 80% similar. |
+| `failed` | The check failed, the replay hit a budget ceiling, or it produced no result to compare. |
+
+Anything that is not `equivalent` is listed for human review and is never accepted automatically. One replay is one sample, since agent runs vary: repeat a replay before acting on a borderline result. Turns are not recorded by the agent runtime, so they are not compared. The `--check` command runs with no timeout, so keep it fast. Use `--json` on `list` and `report` for scripting.
+
+### Viewing aggregate stats
+
+```bash
+ta stats velocity                            # aggregate + per-contributor + conflict warnings
+ta stats velocity --since 2026-01-01         # since a date
+ta stats velocity --phase-prefix 0.15        # only v0.15.x phases
+ta stats velocity --json                     # raw JSON (includes by_contributor, phase_conflicts)
+```
+
+`ta stats velocity` merges both files (dedup by goal_id) and always shows three sections:
+
+1. **Overall aggregate** — totals, outcomes, build time percentiles, total cost
+2. **Per-contributor breakdown** — from committed history entries only (requires `velocity-history.jsonl`)
+3. **Phase conflict warnings** — plan phases where more than one person submitted work
+
+Example output:
+
+```
+Velocity Stats
+──────────────────────────────────────────────────
+  Total goals:        42
+  Applied:            38 (90%)
+  Denied:             2 (5%)
+  Cancelled:          1 (2%)
+  Failed:             1 (2%)
+──────────────────────────────────────────────────
+  Avg build time:     14m 10s
+  P90 build time:     28m 5s
+  Avg rework time:    2m 0s
+  Total rework:       1h 24m
+  Total cost:         $12.45
+  Avg cost/goal:      $0.33
+
+CONTRIBUTOR       GOALS   APPLIED   AVG BUILD     COST
+──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+alice                28        25     12m 30s    $8.20
+bob                  10         9     18m 45s    $3.15
+a3f1bc20              4         4      9m 10s    $1.10
+
+⚠  Phase conflicts detected (1 phase):
+   v0.15.7 — 3 entries from: alice, bob
+   Review these phases to ensure work is not being duplicated.
+```
+
+Phase conflicts flag any plan phase where committed entries exist from more than one contributor — useful for catching parallel work before it causes merge problems.
+
+### Per-goal breakdown
+
+```bash
+ta stats velocity-detail                        # latest 50 goals (merged)
+ta stats velocity-detail --limit 20             # show fewer
+ta stats velocity-detail --phase-prefix 0.15    # only v0.15.x phases
+ta stats velocity-detail --expand-followups     # show rework rows indented under parent
+ta stats velocity-detail --since 2026-03-01 --json
+```
+
+The `SOURCE` column shows `shared` for entries present in the committed history or `[local]` for entries that exist only on this machine. The `FOLLOWUPS` column shows how many follow-up goals were applied against each goal (useful for identifying which phases needed rework).
+
+### Shell velocity widget
+
+In the interactive shell (`ta shell`), type `:stats` to see a live velocity summary inline without leaving the session:
+
+```
+:stats
+```
+
+Output is printed directly into the shell output panel. It shows the same aggregate + contributor breakdown as `ta stats velocity`.
+
+After every `ta draft apply`, a one-line velocity summary is printed in the post-apply status block showing build time and, when available, cost.
+
+### Studio Velocity Dashboard
+
+The Studio UI exposes two API endpoints for the velocity dashboard:
+
+| Endpoint | Query params |
+|----------|-------------|
+| `GET /api/stats/velocity` | `?since=YYYY-MM-DD`, `?phase_prefix=0.15` |
+| `GET /api/stats/velocity-detail` | `?since=YYYY-MM-DD`, `?phase_prefix=0.15`, `?limit=50` |
+
+Both endpoints merge local and committed history, apply optional filters, and return JSON. The `/api/stats/velocity` response includes `aggregate`, `by_contributor`, `phase_conflicts`, and `local_only_count` fields.
+
+### Rework tracking
+
+When a follow-up goal is applied against a parent goal, the parent's entry is updated with:
+- `follow_up_count` — number of follow-up goals applied
+- `rework_seconds` — total build time of all follow-up goals
+
+This lets you see which phases required extra work and how much time was spent on corrections.
+
+### Migrating local history to shared
+
+`ta draft apply` handles migration automatically. The manual `ta stats migrate` command is retained for legacy use (e.g., importing entries from before automatic migration was added):
+
+```bash
+ta stats migrate --dry-run    # preview what would be promoted
+ta stats migrate              # promote all local-only entries (deprecated: now automatic)
+```
+
+### Exporting history
+
+```bash
+ta stats export               # JSON (default, merged view)
+ta stats export --format csv  # CSV for spreadsheets
+ta stats export --committed-only --format csv  # only committed history
+```
+
+The CSV export includes `machine_id`, `committer`, `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, and `cost_usd` columns in addition to the standard fields.
